.TH MKDIR 1
.SH NAME
mkdir  \-  make a directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkdir
.I dirname ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mkdir
creates specified directories
in mode 777.
Standard entries,
.RB ` . ',
for the directory itself,
and
.BR ` .. '
for its parent, are made automatically.
.PP
.I Mkdir
requires write permission in the parent directory.
.PP
.B Note:
the mode is 777 as modified by the
.IR umask (2)
of the calling process, which is what
.I maknode
in the kernel applies to every
.IR mknod (2).
The initial mask,
.B CMASK
in <sys/param.h>, is 0 on this system and nothing sets another, so a directory
made here really is 777 today.
.PP
.B Note:
.I mkdir
is
.B set\-user\-id to root
on this system, and must be: there is no
.I mkdir
system call, so the directory is built by hand out of
.IR mknod (2)
and two
.IR link (2)s,
and all three are refused to anyone but the super\-user.
The bit is granted in
.I root.manifest
at the top of the source tree, as mode 04755.
.PP
The permission the paragraph above speaks of is therefore the program's own:
.I mkdir
calls
.IR access (2)
on the parent directory before it does anything else, and
.I access
asks about the
.B real
user id.  The super\-user rights are borrowed for the three calls that need them
and for nothing else, and the finished directory is handed to the real user and
group with
.IR chown (2).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
rm(1),
mknod(2),
link(2),
chown(2),
umask(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Mkdir
returns exit code 0 if all directories were successfully made.
Otherwise it prints a diagnostic and returns nonzero.
.PP
.B Note:
a name longer than 124 characters is refused rather than copied into a fixed
buffer, which is what v7 did.
